# atomium.sc
#
# Claude prompt (Opus 5.5, Extra High):
# Create the atomium.
# Make the spheres reflective. The environment should match the real world.
# Put the camera close enough so the Atoumium fill the scene but all the parts remain visible.
#
# The Atomium in Brussels (1958): nine 18 m stainless steel spheres at the corners and centre of a
# cube standing on one corner, an iron crystal cell magnified 165 billion times. The cube edge is
# 47 m between sphere centres, which gives the 29 m edge tubes and 23 m diagonal tubes, 3.3 m wide.
# The top sphere ends at 102 m. Three bipods, standing 50 m from the axis, hold up the three lower
# outer spheres, and the bottom sphere rests on the round entrance pavilion (26 m across).
# Around it: the concrete esplanade, lawns and trees of Heysel park, the boulevard leading to the
# Centenary Palace behind the viewer (seen in the spheres), a distant skyline, and a summer sky.
#
# Modelling notes, because the renderer can't rotate objects:
# - The slanted tubes are chains of overlapping small spheres, the slanted bipod legs stacks of short
#   vertical cylinders. Only the vertical tubes of the central axis are real cylinders.
# - The sky is a huge sphere with a negative scale: the renderer doesn't transform normals, so its
#   normals point inwards and the lights inside can light it. Without it, the mirror spheres would
#   reflect the renderer's flat grey background.
# - Reflections use the flat triangle normals, so the spheres reflect in facets, much like the
#   triangular steel panels of the real cladding.

